00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:03.790
takes to orchestrate an API that
you submit against our control

00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:04.819
plane.

00:01:04.819 --> 00:01:08.990
So I've picked a canonical
example of submitting

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:11.210
an instance creation request.

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:15.380
So that API, we
orchestrate that API.

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:18.690
That API has many, many
steps to be performed.

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:22.177
So I just listed a
few of the steps.

00:01:22.177 --> 00:01:23.510
We need to check the permission.

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:25.670
We need to check the
[? org ?] policy,

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:28.700
[? coda, ?] so on and so forth.

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:30.380
Any one of these
steps could fail.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:35.900
And you can imagine
control plane

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:39.500
with many projects,
many resources

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:41.990
that we manage, many APIs
that we need to process.

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:48.739
We need to process and
remember where we are exactly

00:01:48.739 --> 00:01:50.030
so we can recover our failures.

00:01:54.880 --> 00:02:00.430
And since this talk is
about the availability,

00:02:00.430 --> 00:02:03.060
let's define what
is availability.

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:06.490
So our SRE reliability
workbook defines availability

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:09.780
as an uptime of a
software service.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:13.470
And the way we calculate
our availability

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:19.640
is by accumulating all the
unavailable periods of downtime

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:23.410
over some window of time.

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:26.940
So as an example,
four-nines implies

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:31.542
your service was unavailable
over a 30-day for 4.3 minutes,

00:02:31.542 --> 00:02:32.500
and so on and so forth.

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:41.860
There are a couple of
dimensions to availability

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:42.940
for a GCE system.

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:51.550
The VN uptime availability,
this is nothing

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:56.430
but your VMs are programmed,
the network is being configured,

00:02:56.430 --> 00:03:00.790
your PD is configured,
and all the other stuff

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:04.180
has been programmed
by the control plane,

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:10.810
and your resources are
now serving live traffic.

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:13.547
That we call VM
uptime availability.

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:18.570
Just to draw some
distinction, we

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:23.580
have control plane
availability, which is basically

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:26.550
an on-demand control plane
programming that happens

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:30.240
via one of our REST APIs.

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:34.890
And at a very high level,
the control plane programming

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:37.820
centers are on three
key resources--

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:42.780
the VM-- the Virtual
Machines; the PDs;

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:46.515
and any kind of network
access you configure.

00:03:46.515 --> 00:03:48.390
And there are many more
resources, of course,

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:50.560
and I haven't listed them.

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:53.500
So it's important to remember
the distinction about these two

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:54.580
availability things.

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:58.470
The first one is live
traffic serving paths.

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:01.020
The second is control paths.

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:09.470
We're so proud of
the availability

00:04:09.470 --> 00:04:15.590
work they have done that we have
a published SLA for VM uptime.

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:23.540
So for VMs that leverage
our multiple region,

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:28.060
so, for example, VMs placed in
two or more zones in a region

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:31.240
or it could be split
across region--

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:36.820
one can expect an aggregate
uptime availability four-nines.

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:40.760
And this would be with no
loss of external connectivity

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:42.990
or access to persistent disk.

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:51.300
Most importantly, this will be
without any kind of downtime,

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:54.930
and will be transferring
even if we carry out

00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:56.800
any kind of host maintenance.

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:00.300
So if you have to carry out
any kind of security patches,

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:05.582
we'll do that transparently
by live migrating your VMs.

00:05:05.582 --> 00:05:07.290
And in most cases,
you won't notice that.

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:12.190
VIKAS YADAV: So in
the previous slide,

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:13.648
the security patches
we are talking

00:05:13.648 --> 00:05:15.970
about it is the
underlying system on top

00:05:15.970 --> 00:05:18.050
of which the VMs run.

00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:20.740
And we do line migration
when we are patching

00:05:20.740 --> 00:05:22.810
the host on which the VM run.

