00:06:49.570 --> 00:06:51.010
The rest of the
presentation we're

00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:54.460
going to talk through
some of the design

00:06:54.460 --> 00:06:56.710
points that have
worked really well

00:06:56.710 --> 00:07:01.150
as we work to offer a highly
available control plane.

00:07:01.150 --> 00:07:05.740
And this is the system that
offers all the API we serve.

00:07:11.540 --> 00:07:16.530
So I need to introduce some
key building block foundations

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:18.680
before we get started.

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:22.880
So this is a GCE
resource data model.

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:33.150
Resources like VMs and
disks are zonal resources.

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:36.200
Sub-networks, static
IPs, regional disks--

00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:38.780
these are shared
resources, but they're

00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:40.300
shared across within a region.

00:07:44.230 --> 00:07:47.680
Global resources-- the
most important ones

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:51.940
are images, Snapshots,
and InstanceTemplate.

00:07:51.940 --> 00:07:57.200
They are shared across
globally by all the regions.

00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:01.300
So you can imagine this, in
a very object-oriented sense,

00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:05.394
you have a hierarchical
access pattern.

00:08:05.394 --> 00:08:06.560
And we'll talk through this.

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:08.880
And we'll refer
to this data model

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:11.297
as we explain the
kind of choices

00:08:11.297 --> 00:08:13.380
we made to build a highly
available control plane.

00:08:16.280 --> 00:08:18.380
So Vikas, you should
just jump in as--

00:08:18.380 --> 00:08:21.840
VIKAS YADAV: Yeah, I want to
spend some more time here.

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:23.360
So we have a
concept of resources

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:25.110
which are available globally.

00:08:25.110 --> 00:08:29.460
So what that means is if you
have an instance template which

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:32.799
you want to use to launch
VMs in multiple regions,

00:08:32.799 --> 00:08:35.280
you are to define
it only once, and it

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:39.690
will be available in your entire
project across all the regions.

00:08:39.690 --> 00:08:42.330
You don't have to create
a separate template

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:44.130
for a different region.

00:08:44.130 --> 00:08:47.790
So in some sense, this provide
simplicity of management

00:08:47.790 --> 00:08:51.450
and a single pane of control.

00:08:51.450 --> 00:08:53.130
And similarly, there
are some resources

00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:55.080
which are region
specific, which are used

00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:57.690
by all the zones in the region.

00:08:57.690 --> 00:08:59.250
And they follow
a natural pattern

00:08:59.250 --> 00:09:03.390
like IP addresses, which are
local to the region, which

00:09:03.390 --> 00:09:07.620
can be used within the
zones in the region.

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:14.385
And this natural hierarchy
of sharding of the data model

00:09:14.385 --> 00:09:16.320
helps us achieve
high availability,

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:19.172
as we will talk about in the
rest of the presentation.

00:09:19.172 --> 00:09:20.630
RAJESH MISHRA: So
some of this will

00:09:20.630 --> 00:09:23.630
start to make a lot of
sense as we dive deeper

00:09:23.630 --> 00:09:26.967
into the rest of the discussion.

00:09:29.830 --> 00:09:35.740
So we could have chosen a design
to have control plane sorted

00:09:35.740 --> 00:09:38.430
out of a single
scope, have everything

00:09:38.430 --> 00:09:40.020
be global with a
[? sorted out ?]

00:09:40.020 --> 00:09:42.569
global state database.

00:09:42.569 --> 00:09:44.610
But as we know, that's a
single point of failure.

00:09:47.190 --> 00:09:48.930
This could work
as an improvement

00:09:48.930 --> 00:09:50.580
over the previous design.

00:09:50.580 --> 00:09:53.670
So what you see is each
individual scopes, all

00:09:53.670 --> 00:09:57.970
the scope data model
that I talked about,

00:09:57.970 --> 00:10:01.470
resources that are in
the individual scope,

00:10:01.470 --> 00:10:04.780
they're sorted out of the
individual control planes.

00:10:04.780 --> 00:10:07.930
However, in this case,
we're sharing a database.

00:10:07.930 --> 00:10:12.510
That, again, is a
single point of failure.

00:10:12.510 --> 00:10:14.580
One key point here
that I talk about

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:23.380
from a topology standpoint,
we have a frontend--

00:10:23.380 --> 00:10:26.840
we have our Google
frontend, which

00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:30.500
is nothing but a service
router, and there

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:32.480
is a second level
of router we have,

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:36.170
which I've called it
here as API dispatcher.

00:10:36.170 --> 00:10:40.070
And all it does is
it's a scope parser.

00:10:40.070 --> 00:10:44.270
So in our URL path, all
our resources are scoped.

00:10:44.270 --> 00:10:47.450
So you could reference,
let's say, a disk.

00:10:47.450 --> 00:10:50.710
And a disk, as we know,
is a [? zonoscope. ?]

00:10:50.710 --> 00:10:53.360
So based on the
specific zonoscope

00:10:53.360 --> 00:10:56.330
that you are interested
in, the right control plane

00:10:56.330 --> 00:10:58.497
will be involved here.

00:10:58.497 --> 00:11:00.580
It's important to also
call out that there is only

00:11:00.580 --> 00:11:03.100
one singleton global scope.

00:11:03.100 --> 00:11:07.690
And we serve 17 regions, so
there are 17 regional scopes.

00:11:07.690 --> 00:11:10.910
And likewise, there are--

00:11:10.910 --> 00:11:12.965
and I just made a very
gross generalization

00:11:12.965 --> 00:11:17.210
that there are three
zones per region,

00:11:17.210 --> 00:11:23.422
so about 51 independent control
planes serving resources

00:11:23.422 --> 00:11:24.130
within the scope.

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:28.370
So this is the final design.

00:11:28.370 --> 00:11:30.630
This is what we
have in production.

00:11:30.630 --> 00:11:32.000
This is what we use.

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:33.980
We have independent
control plane

00:11:33.980 --> 00:11:38.060
with their own
independent databases.

00:11:38.060 --> 00:11:42.170
VIKAS YADAV: So the theme here
is if you want availability,

00:11:42.170 --> 00:11:46.340
you need to minimize
shared point of failures.

00:11:46.340 --> 00:11:50.210
So the slide number one, there's
a single stack, which is not

00:11:50.210 --> 00:11:52.250
going to work for availability.

00:11:52.250 --> 00:11:55.070
Second slide, things
are independent,

00:11:55.070 --> 00:11:58.160
but there is a shared
database, which,

00:11:58.160 --> 00:12:01.280
again, reduces availability.

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:04.620
Having independent
controls stacks increases

00:12:04.620 --> 00:12:07.970
the aggregate availability,
because there is no shared

00:12:07.970 --> 00:12:10.380
fate between those stacks.

00:12:10.380 --> 00:12:12.500
And that's what we
have zeroed in on,

00:12:12.500 --> 00:12:14.750
that we are not going
to have shared fate

00:12:14.750 --> 00:12:17.570
across the different stacks, and
have multiple different stacks

00:12:17.570 --> 00:12:19.140
for different scopes.

00:12:23.580 --> 00:12:27.900
RAJESH MISHRA: Another key
point that we felt was sharing.

00:12:27.900 --> 00:12:31.320
As we design our system,
what about config?

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:37.600
And what about updating all the
scope control plane binaries?

00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:43.510
The model we have chosen is we
do multi-day phased rollout.

00:12:43.510 --> 00:12:46.420
And this is largely
to limit blast radius.

00:12:46.420 --> 00:12:48.130
So there are some
config that needs

00:12:48.130 --> 00:12:52.240
to be rolled out globally
across all the control planes

00:12:52.240 --> 00:12:55.960
that we serve, and we do this
in a very staged fashion.

00:12:55.960 --> 00:12:59.870
We have circuit
breakers along the way,

00:12:59.870 --> 00:13:04.090
and we do a lot of monitoring
as we tier the rollout.

00:13:04.090 --> 00:13:06.990
So we watch rollout.

00:13:06.990 --> 00:13:09.300
We do zone by zone roll out.

00:13:09.300 --> 00:13:14.460
And based on the success,
we will take it forward.

00:13:14.460 --> 00:13:19.020
And over some short
period of time,

00:13:19.020 --> 00:13:20.440
the rollouts are completed.

00:13:20.440 --> 00:13:23.460
And then you get a new
version of control plane

00:13:23.460 --> 00:13:26.010
if there are any config changes.

00:13:26.010 --> 00:13:28.980
We do a lot of
[? offerant ?] validation.

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:32.150
We presubmit testing
and staging testing.

00:13:35.947 --> 00:13:37.530
So that's our design
point number two.

00:13:41.980 --> 00:13:43.630
Let's spend some
time on something

00:13:43.630 --> 00:13:46.780
that we have internally
started to call Rule of Nine.

00:13:46.780 --> 00:13:49.690
And what this, simply stated--

00:13:49.690 --> 00:13:54.610
assume you're trying to provide
a service called Service A,

00:13:54.610 --> 00:13:56.530
and it's serving a critical API.

00:13:56.530 --> 00:14:01.200
This could be your GCE system
serving CreateInstance API.

00:14:04.380 --> 00:14:10.230
And Service A is built on a lot
of internal services we have--

00:14:10.230 --> 00:14:14.520
identity as an example,
an access check.

00:14:14.520 --> 00:14:17.070
A lot of these
underlying dependencies

00:14:17.070 --> 00:14:19.992
need to offer at
least an availability

00:14:19.992 --> 00:14:21.450
that is higher than
what I'm trying

00:14:21.450 --> 00:14:24.270
to provide to our customers.

00:14:24.270 --> 00:14:27.290
And you can imagine, end
layer of dependencies

00:14:27.290 --> 00:14:30.814
and will go higher
at every layer below.

00:14:30.814 --> 00:14:31.980
And that's the Rule of Nine.

00:14:31.980 --> 00:14:34.629
So we followed this very
diligently by inspecting

00:14:34.629 --> 00:14:36.420
all the [? differences ?]
across the board.

00:14:40.280 --> 00:14:41.780
So that's design
point number three.

00:14:45.240 --> 00:14:49.400
The subsequent discussion--
since this talk

00:14:49.400 --> 00:14:53.320
is about distributed systems
and serving control plane

00:14:53.320 --> 00:14:57.230
availability, let's
spend a minute

00:14:57.230 --> 00:15:00.270
to talk about CAP Theorem.

00:15:00.270 --> 00:15:06.010
So CAP Theorem stated there
are three key factors or three

00:15:06.010 --> 00:15:09.830
key design consideration points
in any distributed system

00:15:09.830 --> 00:15:11.080
design.

00:15:11.080 --> 00:15:15.770
So distributed systems
are partitioned by nature.

00:15:15.770 --> 00:15:18.080
So that's how much
partition tolerance

00:15:18.080 --> 00:15:21.770
you need to build
for your use cases.

00:15:21.770 --> 00:15:25.310
Availability and consistency
are the other two parameters

00:15:25.310 --> 00:15:28.030
that you can play with.

00:15:28.030 --> 00:15:32.660
CAP Theorem states that you
can pick only two of these,

00:15:32.660 --> 00:15:36.740
and you're probably trading
off on the third one.

00:15:36.740 --> 00:15:41.050
And that's what in the
intersection of all three,

00:15:41.050 --> 00:15:44.250
that's almost an
impossible thing to build.

00:15:44.250 --> 00:15:47.270
So you could either end
up with a system that

00:15:47.270 --> 00:15:48.782
is either consistent
and available

00:15:48.782 --> 00:15:50.240
at the same time--
that's typically

00:15:50.240 --> 00:15:56.810
all the old-school single
server model, or more than often

00:15:56.810 --> 00:15:59.090
in a distributed
system, availability

00:15:59.090 --> 00:16:04.070
is extremely important, which
has been the kind of choices

00:16:04.070 --> 00:16:06.260
that we made for a
lot of our design.

00:16:06.260 --> 00:16:09.140
And we picked AP-based model.

00:16:09.140 --> 00:16:11.870
In some cases, you might have
consistency and partition

00:16:11.870 --> 00:16:16.940
tolerance if it's a
shared global object.

00:16:16.940 --> 00:16:19.855
And we'll talk about
that subsequently.

00:16:22.530 --> 00:16:26.970
So here what I have,
an example, where

00:16:26.970 --> 00:16:31.050
we do some kind of lazy
state reconciliation.

00:16:31.050 --> 00:16:35.030
So imagine there is a
critical API being served out

00:16:35.030 --> 00:16:41.850
of your service, and there is
a dependency layer below it.

00:16:41.850 --> 00:16:46.780
You could pick a choice
where you fail-close,

00:16:46.780 --> 00:16:52.060
and you need to reconcile
the state synchronously.

00:16:52.060 --> 00:16:56.460
In a partitioned system
this can be tricky.

00:16:56.460 --> 00:16:59.360
And for some cases,
we felt instead

00:16:59.360 --> 00:17:02.220
of improving availability
it was acceptable to just do

00:17:02.220 --> 00:17:03.283
a fail-open.

00:17:03.283 --> 00:17:05.449
VIKAS YADAV: Yeah, so one
example I'll bring up here

00:17:05.449 --> 00:17:08.460
is, in your application
as well, if you

00:17:08.460 --> 00:17:10.819
have high availability
for your API,

00:17:10.819 --> 00:17:16.319
but you have a dependency which
is not a critical dependency

00:17:16.319 --> 00:17:18.826
and you can live with
fail-open semantics,

00:17:18.826 --> 00:17:20.700
then you don't have to
force the Rule of Nine

00:17:20.700 --> 00:17:22.530
on the dependency downstream.

00:17:22.530 --> 00:17:24.329
For example, let's say, logging.

00:17:24.329 --> 00:17:26.910
You don't have to
require logging

00:17:26.910 --> 00:17:29.800
have higher nines
of availability

00:17:29.800 --> 00:17:33.630
if you can reconcile, build
a reconcile in your system

00:17:33.630 --> 00:17:36.990
that if logging is not
available you still continue

00:17:36.990 --> 00:17:38.435
and reconcile later.

00:17:38.435 --> 00:17:39.810
So that's some of
the patterns we

00:17:39.810 --> 00:17:44.020
have used for non-critical
dependency, where we continue

00:17:44.020 --> 00:17:48.000
the operation, continue the
top level API available,

00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:48.960
and reconcile later.

00:17:52.169 --> 00:17:54.460
RAJESH MISHRA: So that's our
design point number four--

00:17:57.300 --> 00:18:00.830
do a fail-open and do
lazy reconciliation.

00:18:00.830 --> 00:18:06.315
So in some ways this is like
an eventual reconciliation.

00:18:09.050 --> 00:18:12.050
Let's talk a bit about
InstanceTemplate.

00:18:12.050 --> 00:18:13.730
Remember, in our
data model, I talked

00:18:13.730 --> 00:18:18.720
about InstanceTemplate and
Snapshots are global resources.

00:18:18.720 --> 00:18:19.940
So what about them?

00:18:19.940 --> 00:18:23.130
Does that pose a problem
for a GCE system?

00:18:23.130 --> 00:18:25.509
So I did a deep dive
on InstanceTemplate.

00:18:25.509 --> 00:18:27.050
VIKAS YADAV: Yeah,
the reason we want

00:18:27.050 --> 00:18:29.600
to talk about
InstanceTemplate is, earlier

00:18:29.600 --> 00:18:32.840
we showed you that we have
a separate control stack

00:18:32.840 --> 00:18:36.890
for every scope, and there
is a single global scope.

00:18:36.890 --> 00:18:38.720
So InstanceTemplate
are global resources

00:18:38.720 --> 00:18:40.860
which are sorted out
of a single stack.

00:18:40.860 --> 00:18:43.340
So it's important
for us to explain

00:18:43.340 --> 00:18:46.550
how we achieve high availability
for InstanceTemplate

00:18:46.550 --> 00:18:47.190
and Snapshot.

00:18:47.190 --> 00:18:48.773
So Rajesh is going
to talk about that.

00:18:52.150 --> 00:18:53.950
RAJESH MISHRA: So
InstanceTemplate

00:18:53.950 --> 00:18:56.640
is something that
gets configured.

00:18:56.640 --> 00:18:59.710
So this is a typical workflow
of an InstanceTemplate.

00:18:59.710 --> 00:19:01.340
You create an InstanceTemplate.

00:19:01.340 --> 00:19:04.520
It's a global resource.

00:19:04.520 --> 00:19:08.750
And typically a
managed instance group

00:19:08.750 --> 00:19:13.870
depends on referencing an
InstanceTemplate, which

00:19:13.870 --> 00:19:17.974
is then, in, turn
used by VM creation

00:19:17.974 --> 00:19:19.390
within the managed
instance group.

00:19:22.570 --> 00:19:26.920
So it's a global resource, and
it creates a global dependency

00:19:26.920 --> 00:19:31.630
for something that is a
regional API or a zonal API.

00:19:31.630 --> 00:19:33.595
This could be a problem.

00:19:36.640 --> 00:19:39.040
So how does autoscaling
or auto healing

00:19:39.040 --> 00:19:43.690
within the context of a
managed instance group work?

00:19:43.690 --> 00:19:46.900
So by the way, there is
a managed instance group.

00:19:46.900 --> 00:19:49.350
Folks are doing a
presentation later today.

00:19:49.350 --> 00:19:51.100
They will talk a little
bit in more detail

00:19:51.100 --> 00:19:54.310
about regional scaling,
regional autoscaling

00:19:54.310 --> 00:19:58.420
and regional managed
instance group.

00:19:58.420 --> 00:20:02.230
What's very key for both
autoscaling and auto healing,

00:20:02.230 --> 00:20:04.690
you need to be able to create
instances and reference

00:20:04.690 --> 00:20:07.240
your template.

00:20:07.240 --> 00:20:10.030
So the solution
we've arrived, we

00:20:10.030 --> 00:20:12.400
cache InstanceTemplate
in all regions.

00:20:12.400 --> 00:20:15.570
We actually do two
levels of caching.

00:20:15.570 --> 00:20:17.950
The intuition that
we operated on

00:20:17.950 --> 00:20:21.880
is that it was acceptable to
go with eventual consistency

00:20:21.880 --> 00:20:23.690
with an AP-based model.

00:20:23.690 --> 00:20:27.550
So we just talked about CAP,
and this is the AP model

00:20:27.550 --> 00:20:33.460
that we could successfully
apply for InstanceTemplate.

00:20:33.460 --> 00:20:36.610
VIKAS YADAV: So the
availability of InstanceTemplate

00:20:36.610 --> 00:20:39.110
is high because we cache it.

00:20:39.110 --> 00:20:43.410
And what that means is
if a single global scope

00:20:43.410 --> 00:20:47.310
or single global stack has a
problem, the InstanceTemplates

00:20:47.310 --> 00:20:49.680
which are cached with
the regions and zones,

00:20:49.680 --> 00:20:52.491
they are available for
autoscaling and auto healing

00:20:52.491 --> 00:20:52.990
to work.

00:20:56.230 --> 00:20:58.600
RAJESH MISHRA: And there
are more nuances to this.

00:20:58.600 --> 00:21:00.890
And I'm not talking
about all the use cases.

00:21:00.890 --> 00:21:02.980
So there are nuances
of, you're trying

00:21:02.980 --> 00:21:07.840
to delete an InstanceTemplate,
so what then?

00:21:07.840 --> 00:21:10.870
And we can cover that
if there is interest

00:21:10.870 --> 00:21:12.835
later in the presentation.

00:21:17.530 --> 00:21:18.030
OK.

00:21:18.030 --> 00:21:19.740
I built this twice.

00:21:19.740 --> 00:21:20.980
My apologies.

00:21:26.160 --> 00:21:30.050
So let's spend a
minute on Snapshot.

00:21:30.050 --> 00:21:31.980
So Snapshot is another
global resource.

00:21:36.150 --> 00:21:38.362
Snapshots are
taken on your disk,

00:21:38.362 --> 00:21:39.445
which is a zonal resource.

00:21:45.840 --> 00:21:48.990
However, there is something
that we did that we never

00:21:48.990 --> 00:21:50.850
exposed to customers.

00:21:50.850 --> 00:21:54.540
The underlying
snapshot has always

00:21:54.540 --> 00:21:59.370
been a multiregional concept,
except the Snapshot resource

00:21:59.370 --> 00:22:01.395
itself, the metadata has
been a global resource.

00:22:03.970 --> 00:22:07.710
The GCS buckets where the
physical raw snapshots

00:22:07.710 --> 00:22:11.280
are stored, it's a
multiregional concept.

00:22:11.280 --> 00:22:12.900
VIKAS YADAV: So
this is important.

00:22:12.900 --> 00:22:17.760
If you have a zonal disk,
and you take a snapshot,

00:22:17.760 --> 00:22:21.420
you don't have to worry about
that zone not being available

00:22:21.420 --> 00:22:24.060
and you not being able
to access your snapshot.

00:22:24.060 --> 00:22:27.780
So the metadata for
Snapshot is global,

00:22:27.780 --> 00:22:30.490
and it's available via
eventual consistency

00:22:30.490 --> 00:22:33.010
in all the other regions.

00:22:33.010 --> 00:22:36.720
The GCE bucket where the
snapshot data is stored,

00:22:36.720 --> 00:22:39.990
that is multiregional,
and that provides

00:22:39.990 --> 00:22:42.720
higher availability for the
actual Snapshot content,

00:22:42.720 --> 00:22:45.750
and high availability for
the Snapshot metadata.

00:22:45.750 --> 00:22:49.260
So you can imagine a scenario
where your zonal snapshot,

00:22:49.260 --> 00:22:52.820
once the zone has trouble,
you can use the snapshot

00:22:52.820 --> 00:22:54.620
and utilize that in
some of those zone.

00:23:00.245 --> 00:23:02.120
RAJESH MISHRA: So Vikas
already covered this.

00:23:05.430 --> 00:23:09.280
There's another thing
that we announced

00:23:09.280 --> 00:23:16.710
a GCP Next, which has
been in an alpha mode

00:23:16.710 --> 00:23:18.490
for some of our customers.

00:23:18.490 --> 00:23:22.920
We're now going to expose
the snapshot locality.

00:23:22.920 --> 00:23:26.850
So as I told you, the underlying
multiregional locality

00:23:26.850 --> 00:23:27.799
will get exposed.

00:23:27.799 --> 00:23:30.090
There was a new parameter
we're exposing called storage

00:23:30.090 --> 00:23:32.700
locations.

00:23:32.700 --> 00:23:35.310
So you'll be able to learn where
your physical snapshots are

00:23:35.310 --> 00:23:37.710
getting stored.

00:23:37.710 --> 00:23:40.400
Additionally, we're
going to follow--

00:23:40.400 --> 00:23:44.760
we're going to allow you
providing choices for where

00:23:44.760 --> 00:23:46.900
your snapshot will get stored.

00:23:46.900 --> 00:23:49.560
So this can be done
on your request body

00:23:49.560 --> 00:23:54.000
by specifying the specific
multiregional place where

00:23:54.000 --> 00:23:56.670
you need to get
your snapshot stored

00:23:56.670 --> 00:24:00.540
if you don't want
us to choose, or you

00:24:00.540 --> 00:24:02.874
could specify specific regions.

00:24:02.874 --> 00:24:04.290
So you could have
your source disk

00:24:04.290 --> 00:24:07.730
in the US in any
one of the zonal--

00:24:07.730 --> 00:24:09.300
US zones.

00:24:09.300 --> 00:24:13.560
And you can pick your regional
destination to be in Europe.

00:24:16.136 --> 00:24:17.260
This is what it looks like.

00:24:20.850 --> 00:24:21.940
That's a parameter.

00:24:21.940 --> 00:24:24.920
So I've given two examples--

00:24:24.920 --> 00:24:27.750
storage locations with
just one location, or you

00:24:27.750 --> 00:24:29.670
pick a multiregional thing.

00:24:29.670 --> 00:24:33.880
So the multiregional
is a GCS construct.

00:24:33.880 --> 00:24:37.835
EU simply implies it's
one of the EU regions.

00:24:40.860 --> 00:24:44.001
As I explained in
the previous thing,

00:24:44.001 --> 00:24:50.490
we will tell you what are the
specific multiregional places

00:24:50.490 --> 00:24:52.400
where we place the snapshot on.

00:24:55.160 --> 00:24:59.130
So the design point in both
Snapshot and InstanceTemplate

00:24:59.130 --> 00:25:05.550
that has worked for us is
eventual consistency and UX.

00:25:05.550 --> 00:25:10.590
So let's take a moment
and talk about the UX.

00:25:10.590 --> 00:25:13.200
The choice we
could have taken is

00:25:13.200 --> 00:25:15.900
we could have converted this
into a regional resource.

00:25:15.900 --> 00:25:18.960
We could have deprecated
our existing global resource

00:25:18.960 --> 00:25:22.320
and offered regional
equivalents of those.

00:25:22.320 --> 00:25:25.560
We felt this was a
good user convenience

00:25:25.560 --> 00:25:27.720
to a lot of customers.

00:25:27.720 --> 00:25:31.230
Because oftentimes, you
try to create a snapshot

00:25:31.230 --> 00:25:33.935
and make them available
in multiple regions,

00:25:33.935 --> 00:25:36.060
and you're going to end up
doing the work yourself.

00:25:36.060 --> 00:25:40.230
So we're just saving
the burden by taking

00:25:40.230 --> 00:25:44.070
on a lot of that complexity
as part of the GCE system.

00:25:44.070 --> 00:25:46.170
So the UX was a key point.

00:25:46.170 --> 00:25:47.880
So we didn't want
to trade off on some

00:25:47.880 --> 00:25:50.130
of the user convenience.

00:25:50.130 --> 00:25:52.350
Meanwhile, we could achieve
all the high availability

00:25:52.350 --> 00:25:56.150
characteristics by providing
eventual consistency

00:25:56.150 --> 00:25:59.330
by caching those resources
in each one of the regions.

00:26:01.980 --> 00:26:04.370
So that's our design
point number five.

00:26:04.370 --> 00:26:09.700
So this a recap of all the
five design points we covered.

00:26:09.700 --> 00:26:14.710
And above all, and
most importantly--

00:26:14.710 --> 00:26:17.570
and it's an operational point--

00:26:17.570 --> 00:26:20.600
at Google, most services
we carry out something

00:26:20.600 --> 00:26:22.130
we call DRT.

00:26:22.130 --> 00:26:29.450
DRT is disaster recovery,
and we simulate failures.

00:26:29.450 --> 00:26:31.390
We simulate region
unavailability.

00:26:31.390 --> 00:26:34.100
We simulate latency.

00:26:34.100 --> 00:26:36.950
And we want to make
sure our APIs are well

00:26:36.950 --> 00:26:39.290
behaving across all
those failures that

00:26:39.290 --> 00:26:40.750
could happen in the real world.

00:26:45.474 --> 00:26:46.890
VIKAS YADAV: And
this DRT exercise

00:26:46.890 --> 00:26:49.050
is invisible to the customer.

00:26:49.050 --> 00:26:50.380
we do it internally.

00:26:50.380 --> 00:26:53.170
It's planned, and
we prepare for it.

00:26:53.170 --> 00:26:55.330
And we want to see the
system behavior when

00:26:55.330 --> 00:26:58.100
disaster recovery is going on.

00:26:58.100 --> 00:27:00.200
And this does not impact
any of the external VMs

00:27:00.200 --> 00:27:01.720
or external line traffic.

00:27:04.560 --> 00:27:09.660
RAJESH MISHRA: So so far,
we've discussed our data model

00:27:09.660 --> 00:27:12.975
and the fact that our
resources are either--

00:27:12.975 --> 00:27:15.540
are scope resources,
so either they're

00:27:15.540 --> 00:27:18.870
zonal, regional, or global.

00:27:22.150 --> 00:27:26.800
And you could use control plane
to provision your resources.

00:27:26.800 --> 00:27:30.030
And you could do this
on-demand, in real-time

00:27:30.030 --> 00:27:33.150
as you're serving traffic,
or you could pre-provision.

00:27:33.150 --> 00:27:34.710
So let's apply some lessons.

00:27:34.710 --> 00:27:36.210
So there are two
ways to approach

00:27:36.210 --> 00:27:40.260
this when it comes to
serving real-time traffic

00:27:40.260 --> 00:27:43.480
for critical operations.

00:27:43.480 --> 00:27:47.550
So let's imagine you
built this service.

00:27:47.550 --> 00:27:53.390
You built a GCE application
that serves 1,000 QPS.

00:27:53.390 --> 00:27:56.340
And let's say you did one
of those good best design

00:27:56.340 --> 00:28:03.270
practices that we advise on
GCE, and you leverage two zones,

00:28:03.270 --> 00:28:09.540
in this case, US East
1A and US East 1B.

00:28:09.540 --> 00:28:12.360
In my contrived
example, each VM has

00:28:12.360 --> 00:28:15.750
been tested to serve
100 QPS, and they've

00:28:15.750 --> 00:28:17.130
been tested for that.

00:28:17.130 --> 00:28:22.830
So you have five VMs on the
either side on two zones served

00:28:22.830 --> 00:28:25.590
by isolated controlled planes.

00:28:25.590 --> 00:28:31.280
And even the VMs are completely
isolated because they are

00:28:31.280 --> 00:28:34.290
in two different serving paths.

00:28:37.110 --> 00:28:40.950
Imagine you lose
one of the zones.

00:28:40.950 --> 00:28:44.290
You're going to double
their traffic on US East 1B.

00:28:46.880 --> 00:28:49.470
This may be not desirable,
because you tested your service

00:28:49.470 --> 00:28:51.570
for 100 QPS.

00:28:51.570 --> 00:28:54.540
So the choices you have--

00:28:54.540 --> 00:29:00.620
you would go on-demand
and ask for five more VMs.

00:29:05.880 --> 00:29:08.700
There is a possibility
you may not get five VMs,

00:29:08.700 --> 00:29:11.900
because we may stock out.

00:29:11.900 --> 00:29:16.520
Maybe US West 1A is experiencing
a zone-wide failure,

00:29:16.520 --> 00:29:19.070
and every other customer is
trying to do the same thing.

00:29:19.070 --> 00:29:20.478
That could be problematic.

00:29:26.170 --> 00:29:27.690
So you could pre-provision.

00:29:27.690 --> 00:29:33.920
In pre-provisioning, you
will pre-provision five more

00:29:33.920 --> 00:29:35.690
instances.

00:29:35.690 --> 00:29:39.530
So here you end up
with 10 instances each.

00:29:39.530 --> 00:29:41.690
And in reality, the
traffic will look

00:29:41.690 --> 00:29:43.910
like this in a steady state.

00:29:43.910 --> 00:29:47.210
So you're serving half
what you tested for,

00:29:47.210 --> 00:29:51.440
and that may be a
safe place to be.

00:29:51.440 --> 00:29:56.260
And in the event
you lose a zone,

00:29:56.260 --> 00:29:58.520
you would be safely
able to serve

00:29:58.520 --> 00:30:00.800
twice the amount of
traffic, because that's

00:30:00.800 --> 00:30:01.670
what you tested for.

00:30:05.040 --> 00:30:08.890
So let's recap what we learned.

00:30:08.890 --> 00:30:15.570
So if you pre-provision, you're
done with a control plane,

00:30:15.570 --> 00:30:19.770
configuring your VMs, the
disks, and all the network

00:30:19.770 --> 00:30:23.930
programming that goes with
it, and then, at that point,

00:30:23.930 --> 00:30:26.340
your VM is serving live traffic.

00:30:26.340 --> 00:30:29.700
And we have published
SLA for that,

00:30:29.700 --> 00:30:33.130
and that works out really well.

00:30:33.130 --> 00:30:39.350
So the pros are, this
pattern is predictable.

00:30:39.350 --> 00:30:41.420
It's simple.

00:30:41.420 --> 00:30:44.270
On the downside, it
costs a little bit more,

00:30:44.270 --> 00:30:47.930
and in a steady state
or in the average case,

00:30:47.930 --> 00:30:51.330
your system's a
little less utilized.

00:30:51.330 --> 00:30:53.360
In the on-demand, you
put the control plane

00:30:53.360 --> 00:30:56.440
on the critical path.

00:30:56.440 --> 00:31:01.200
So here you save on utilization,
and that's an advantage.

00:31:01.200 --> 00:31:06.870
That could work out for
some of your use cases.

00:31:06.870 --> 00:31:10.190
But this is unpredictable.

00:31:10.190 --> 00:31:15.620
If it's Black Friday, you
may not get your instances.

00:31:18.982 --> 00:31:20.440
VIKAS YADAV: So
the on-demand model

00:31:20.440 --> 00:31:24.910
is where if you run into a
problem in one of your zones,

00:31:24.910 --> 00:31:27.115
you try to allocate
in other zones

00:31:27.115 --> 00:31:29.680
an equal number of
VMs which you need.

00:31:29.680 --> 00:31:32.440
And what Rajesh is pointing
out, it's not a guaranteed

00:31:32.440 --> 00:31:35.320
unless they are
reserved or provisioned

00:31:35.320 --> 00:31:39.860
that you need x amount of VMs,
you'll get x amount of VMs.

00:31:39.860 --> 00:31:42.060
It could be a
stock-out situation,

00:31:42.060 --> 00:31:44.240
it could be a
thundering herd problem,

00:31:44.240 --> 00:31:47.440
that the zone you want
that's over capacity,

00:31:47.440 --> 00:31:51.046
and nobody can get extra VMs.

00:31:51.046 --> 00:31:53.170
RAJESH MISHRA: So that
concludes our presentations.

00:31:53.170 --> 00:31:59.520
[MUSIC PLAYING]

